    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d328-a3">

                <pb facs="0508" n="466" sameAs="frus:frus1863p1/pg466"/>

                <head>[Forwarded by Andrew A. Harwood, Commandant.]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington Navy
                            Yard</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-11-15"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 15, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> In answer to your question as to the
                    sufficiencies of supplies and stores on board the Sunbeam, I have the honor to
                    state that on her arrival in New York there was sufficient to have enabled her
                    to keep the sea for at least one week longer. Supplies were furnished the
                    Sunbeam for twenty-two men for ten days, and I was four days and a half in
                    making the passage.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>CHARLES FOLSOM, <hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                    Master.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Commander <hi rendition="#small-caps">James F. Armstrong</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Commanding U. S. Steamer State of Georgia,
                            Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
